# Evolve a Word

In this notebook you evolve the four-letter word `"TUNE"` from a population of
wrong guesses. The fitness function and the genetic operators are small enough
to read in full, so you can watch each part of a genetic search: the starting
population, crossover, mutation, generation winners, seeding, and the target
score that ends the run. The notebook runs offline and needs no credentials.

## Setup

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:tune, "~> 0.5.0"}
])
```

## Score a word

A candidate is a plain string. Its score is the number of positions that match
the target word.

```elixir
letters = ~c"TUNE"

score = fn word ->
  word
  |> String.to_charlist()
  |> Enum.zip(letters)
  |> Enum.count(fn {a, b} -> a == b end)
end

Enum.map(["TTTT", "TUNT", "TUNE"], score)
```

```text
[1, 3, 4]
```

## Write the operators

Genetic search over a plain value needs two functions from you. Each one takes
a random state as its last argument and returns `{new_genome, new_random_state}`.
Tune owns the random state and threads it through every call, so a fixed seed
gives a repeatable search.

`crossover` takes two parents and joins the front of one to the back of the
other at a random cut. `mutate` replaces one random position with one of the
target's letters.

```elixir
crossover = fn left, right, rng ->
  {cut, rng} = :rand.uniform_s(3, rng)
  {String.slice(left, 0, cut) <> String.slice(right, cut, 4 - cut), rng}
end

mutate = fn word, rng ->
  {position, rng} = :rand.uniform_s(4, rng)
  {letter, rng} = :rand.uniform_s(4, rng)

  new_word =
    word
    |> String.to_charlist()
    |> List.replace_at(position - 1, Enum.at(letters, letter - 1))
    |> List.to_string()

  {new_word, rng}
end
```

Call them by hand with a seeded `:rand` state to see one step of each:

```elixir
rng = :rand.seed_s(:exsss, 4)
{child, rng} = crossover.("TTTT", "UUUU", rng)
{mutant, _rng} = mutate.("NNNN", rng)
{child, mutant}
```

```text
{"TTUU", "NNNT"}
```

## Build the search

The target `"TTTT"` is the starting value. With no `Tune.vary/2` call, genetic
search treats the whole value as the genome and uses your operators.

- `initial_population` fixes generation zero. Its length sets the population
  size. Duplicates stay, so `"TTTT"` appears twice.
- `mutation_rate: 1` and `crossover_rate: 1` apply both operators to every
  child.
- `target_score: 4` stops the search once a candidate matches all four letters.
- `Tune.limit/2` must set an evaluation limit for genetic search. `seed` fixes
  the random state.

The single validation case `:spell` is a placeholder: the runner `& &1`
returns the candidate word itself, which the score then reads.

```elixir
spelling = fn seed, opts ->
  Tune.new("TTTT", run: & &1, on_progress: Keyword.get(opts, :on_progress))
  |> Tune.data(validation: [:spell])
  |> Tune.score(:matching_positions, score)
  |> Tune.search(:genetic,
    initial_population: ["TTTT", "UUUU", "NNNN", "EEEE", "TTTT"],
    generations: 25,
    mutation_rate: 1,
    crossover_rate: 1,
    crossover: crossover,
    mutate: mutate,
    target_score: Keyword.get(opts, :target_score, 4)
  )
  |> Tune.limit(evaluations: 130, seed: seed)
  |> Tune.tune()
end
```

## Watch each generation

The `on_progress` callback receives a `:generation_evaluated` event after each
generation. The event carries the whole population and the best candidate so
far.

```elixir
report = fn
  %{event: :generation_evaluated} = event ->
    words = Enum.map(event.population, & &1.value)
    IO.puts("Generation #{event.generation}: best #{event.best.value} #{inspect(words)}")

  _event ->
    :ok
end

{:ok, winner} = spelling.(3, on_progress: report)

IO.puts("Selected #{winner.value} after #{length(winner.history)} evaluations")
IO.puts("Stop reason #{winner.stop_reason}")
```

```text
Generation 0: best TTTT ["TTTT", "UUUU", "NNNN", "EEEE", "TTTT"]
Generation 1: best UUNT ["TTTT", "TTTT", "TTTN", "UUNT", "NNNN"]
Generation 2: best TUNT ["UUNT", "EUTT", "TUNT", "UTTE", "UTTN"]
Generation 3: best TUNE ["TUNT", "NUTT", "TUNE", "TUUT", "UUNT"]
Selected TUNE after 20 evaluations
Stop reason target_score
```

The first member of each later generation is an elite: the previous
generation's best candidate, copied unchanged. The default `elitism: 1` keeps
one. The other members are offspring of two parents chosen by tournament.

## Trace the lineage

Every candidate in `winner.history` records its kind, generation, and parents.

```elixir
winner.history
|> Enum.take(6)
|> Enum.map(&{&1.id, &1.value, &1.lineage})
```

```text
[
  {"0", "TTTT", %{kind: :initial, generation: 0}},
  {"1", "UUUU", %{kind: :initial, generation: 0}},
  {"2", "NNNN", %{kind: :initial, generation: 0}},
  {"3", "EEEE", %{kind: :initial, generation: 0}},
  {"4", "TTTT", %{kind: :initial, generation: 0}},
  {"5", "TTTT", %{kind: :elite, parents: ["0"], generation: 1}}
]
```

```elixir
winner.best.lineage
```

```text
%{kind: :offspring, parents: ["12", "13"], generation: 3}
```

The two `"TTTT"` seeds keep separate IDs, `"0"` and `"4"`. The winner is the
child of candidates `"12"` and `"13"` from generation 2.

## Repeat a run with the same seed

The operators draw every random number from the state Tune passes in, so the
same seed gives the same history.

```elixir
{:ok, again} = spelling.(3, [])
{:ok, other} = spelling.(5, [])

IO.puts("Same history with seed 3: #{Enum.map(winner.history, & &1.value) == Enum.map(again.history, & &1.value)}")
IO.puts("Seed 5 found #{other.value} after #{length(other.history)} evaluations")
```

```text
Same history with seed 3: true
Seed 5 found TUNE after 125 evaluations
```

A different seed finds the same word along a longer path.

## Run without a target score

Without `target_score`, the search keeps going until it runs all 25
generations, even though it found the answer in generation 3.

```elixir
{:ok, full} = spelling.(3, target_score: nil)

IO.puts("Selected #{full.value} after #{length(full.history)} evaluations")
IO.puts("Stop reason #{full.stop_reason}")
```

```text
Selected TUNE after 130 evaluations
Stop reason completed
```

Set `target_score` when you know the best possible score. It saves the
remaining evaluations.

